{
  "requirement": "`Criterion::execute` can compute an answer without a configured model provider.\nA criterion can compute its answer directly using tracked inputs; deterministic\nexecution needs no configured agent. Evidence storage is still required.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two obligations. First, `Criterion::execute` can compute an answer from tracked inputs with no model provider. Second, evidence storage is still required.\n\n- **No agent, tracked inputs:** `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#fn[deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent]` builds a `Validator` from only the `Memory` graph, with no agent or model provider. Its `Review` criterion in `impl[Review/Criterion<Memory>]/fn[execute]` reads the node contents through `ctx.inspection()` and returns an `Assessment`. The test asserts `result == false` and `rationale == \"ordinary computation\"`, the node's contents. It also asserts that the saved evidence has a `Dependency::Node(Node(1), fingerprint)` whose fingerprint value is the contents. That shows the answer was computed from a tracked input.\n- **Evidence storage:** the same test loads the evidence back through `graph.load_evidence`. It is present and holds the dependency, so storage was used. The storage requirement is structural: `Validator::new` takes the graph and no agent. The tests supply storage on every path, and nothing in the text asks for a failure case without it.\n- **Supporting tests:** `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_have_independent_typed_answers_and_cache_negative_results]` and `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs#fn[runner_reuses_negative_judgments_and_records_only_callback_observations]` also run deterministic criteria with no agent. They assert recorded outputs, cached negative results, and the number of executions and writes.\n\nI found no plausible violation these tests would accept.",
  "references": "cd19cdc40d9a165ab1cabe10dabb2c8e894a4e3b8d80e67d7b7c247effc70b84",
